        "math_academy_id": "q-102797",
        "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}-6y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$",
        "worked_solution": "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{\u2032}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{\u2033}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{\u2032}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{\u2032}{x}^{4}+6u{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{\u2032}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{\u2033}x+6{u}^{\u2032}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{\u2033}+6{u}^{\u2032}=0.\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
